  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry & chilly tonight with widespread frost outside \nthe urban centers. Sunny & becoming breezy Wed with milder high \ntemps  reaching well into the 60s. Much milder Wed night with S-SW \nwinds holding low temps mainly in the upper 40s-lower 50s. \n\nLarge high pressure builds overhead tonight. This coupled with the \ndry/cool airmass and mainly clear skies will result in good \nradiational cooling and unseasonably chilly temps tonight. Low \ntemperatures should bottom out well down into the 30s in many \nlocations with even some upper 20s in the normally coolest low-lying \nareas of western MA. Frost and even freeze headlines are in effect \nfor much of the region outside the urban heat islands. The mild spot \nwill be downtown Boston...where low temps will only drop into the \nmiddle 40s.\n\nThis high pressure system moves east of the region on Wed. This \nresults in a return southwest flow of milder air to work back into \nthe region. Sunny skies and 925T warming to around +10C by afternoon \nwill allow for a very nice recovery in temps. Highs should reach the \nlower 60s in the high terrain with middle to upper 60s in most other \nlocations. It will become a bit breezy Wed afternoon with Bufkit \nsoundings supporting SW wind gusts of 20-25 mph...but still a very \nnice day. \n\nA modest southwest LLJ of 30-40 knots ahead of an approaching cold \nfront Wed night will prevent surface winds from decoupling. This \nwill result in a much milder low temperatures that what is on tap \nfor tonight. Low temps will only drop into the upper 40s and lower \n50s in most locations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Very low chance of a spot shower Thursday, otherwise \na mild day. An early look at the holiday weekend favors dry weather, \nthough late Sunday into Monday bears watching for rain.\n\nThere aren't many changes to the extended forecast. As previously \nmentioned, a vertically stacked low near Hudson Bay will send a few \nsubtle shortwaves through New England Thursday. Limited moisture \nshould preclude significant rainfall, though a spot shower cannot be \nruled out. The NBM doesn't show this particularly well, but CAMs do \nindicate low-end PoPs during the day Thursday associated with a \nsurface cold front. Southwest flow aloft also brings somewhat \nelevated PWATs into the region. Thus, felt it best to introduce \nSlight Chance (15-20%) PoPs. This is far from a washout, with much \nof the region and day remaining dry, though the setup does favor a \nfew spot showers. Otherwise, dry weather prevails Friday through the \nstart of the holiday weekend.\n\nForecast uncertainty increases late Sunday into Columbus Day and \nwill depend largely on potential tropical development over the Bay \nof Campeche. Development of AL92 is expected, and if named would \nbecome Isaias. Some guidance brings remnant tropical moisture north \ninto southern New England sometime late Sunday night or Monday, \nwhile other solutions keep it suppressed to our south as a strong \n1030 mb high extends across much of southern Canada. Plenty of time \nto watch trends, with no reason to alter outdoor plans at this point.\n\nTemperatures remain fall-like with highs mainly in the middle to \nupper 60s, except upper 60s to mid 70s Thursday. Overnight lows are \nmostly in the upper 40s and 50s, though cooler upper 30s to middle \n40s are possible Friday night into Saturday morning. Even cooler \nconditions are possible where effective radiational cooling develops \nbeneath the surface high.",
